and he possesses the nine expressions of the dance. The body expressions are: graceful, heroic, and terrifying. The speech expressions are: fierce, laughing, and ferocious. The mind expressions are: compassionate, awe-inspiring, and peaceful—these are the nine. Their symbolism and how they are visualized from the syllable <term>E</term> and the <term>dharmodaya</term> will be shown in detail during the actual practice of the <term>sadhana</term> <gloss>liturgy</gloss>, so they are not written here. In the center of his body, the extremely subtle <term>dhuti</term> exists perfectly straight. At the level of his heart is the wisdom-being white <term>Heruka</term> father and mother, along with the heart-life-force syllable <term>HUM</term>. At his crown is the Great Brahmin Sarahapa, white with a reddish tint, with one face and two hands holding a vajra and skull-cup. His legs are in the vajra posture, and he is embraced by the consort Padma Dakini. He is adorned with the six bone ornaments, a tiger-skin loincloth, and so forth. At his crown is <term>OM</term>; at his throat is <term>AH</term>;
